# Review ownership.
#
# A single maintainer owns everything today, so this file buys no routing. It is here for two
# other reasons: the ruleset can require code-owner review without a second commit, and the
# per-path entries below record which parts of the tree are load-bearing enough to deserve a named
# owner once anyone else contributes.
#
# NOTE: CODEOWNERS does not enforce anything by itself. It only takes effect when a ruleset or
# branch protection requires code-owner review. See .github/main-ruleset.json.

# Default owner for anything not matched below.
*                               @m-nweke

# The architectural boundary. posture-core must stay pure — numpy and the standard library only —
# and pose-backends quarantines the one heavy, platform-fragile dependency behind a Protocol. A
# change here can dissolve the property that lets the rules suite run in seconds.
/packages/posture-core/         @m-nweke
/packages/pose-backends/        @m-nweke

# Automation and supply chain. A workflow change can weaken every other gate in the repo, and
# unlike application code its failure mode is silent: gates keep reporting green while checking
# less. Action pins live here too.
/.github/                       @m-nweke

# Architecture decision records are the reasoning of record. Editing one rewrites history rather
# than adding to it; superseding is done by adding a new ADR that says so.
/docs/adr/                      @m-nweke

# Preserved audit evidence. docs/FINDINGS.md cites these files by line number, so they are
# deliberately immutable — excluded from ruff, mypy, Prettier and pre-commit's fixers.
/docs/archive/                  @m-nweke
